{"name":"Sea Anemone","hex":"#e8dad6","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"sea anemone","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any polyp of the order Actiniaria that occurs in marine or estuarine habitats and captures its food using tentacles."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/sea_anemone"}],"entries":[{"title":"Sea anemone","blocks":[{"text":"Sea anemones are a group of predatory marine invertebrate animals constituting the order Actiniaria. Because of their colourful appearance, they are named after the Anemone, a terrestrial flowering plant. Sea anemones are classified in the phylum Cnidaria, class Anthozoa, subclass Hexacorallia.\nAs cnidarians, sea anemones are related to corals, jellyfish, tube-dwelling anemones, and Hydra. Unlike jellyfish, sea anemones do not have a medusa stage in their life cycle."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/76/Actiniaria.jpg/330px-Actiniaria.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sea_anemone","wikidata":"Q147256"},{"title":"Sea","blocks":[{"text":"A sea is a very large body of salty water. The term has two distinct meanings. The first is the interconnected body of seawater that spans most of Earth, known as the sea or the ocean. The second is a named body of water: usually either a marginal sea, or a large, nearly landlocked body of water."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1c/Mediterranean_Sea_16.61811E_38.99124N.jpg/330px-Mediterranean_Sea_16.61811E_38.99124N.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sea","wikidata":"Q165"},{"title":"Anemone","blocks":[{"text":"Anemone is a genus of flowering plants in the buttercup family Ranunculaceae. Plants of the genus are commonly called windflowers. They are native to the temperate and subtropical regions of all regions except Australia, New Zealand, and Antarctica. The genus is closely related to several other genera including Anemonoides, Anemonastrum, Hepatica, and Pulsatilla. Some botanists include these genera within Anemone."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/de/Anemones5.jpg/330px-Anemones5.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Anemone","wikidata":"Q158286"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7a/Actinodendron_arboreum2.jpg/500px-Actinodendron_arboreum2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7a/Actinodendron_arboreum2.jpg/500px-Actinodendron_arboreum2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Actinodendron arboreum, the hell's-fire anemone","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AActinodendron_arboreum2.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/64/Striped_colonial_anemone.jpg/500px-Striped_colonial_anemone.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/64/Striped_colonial_anemone.jpg/500px-Striped_colonial_anemone.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Striped colonial anemone","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AStriped_colonial_anemone.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Brooding_sea_anemone_Epiactis_prolifera_1.jpg/500px-Brooding_sea_anemone_Epiactis_prolifera_1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Brooding_sea_anemone_Epiactis_prolifera_1.jpg/1280px-Brooding_sea_anemone_Epiactis_prolifera_1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/com